#edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483184} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cb65ee-c4ba-5892-87e8-7031e44aace7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd301c04-28fc-586b-bd66-53538190b1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483184} :answer-field/id #uuid "686d35a7-2873-597a-8c04-798dd6392868" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb798f6-eb02-5eb0-b90e-3818bf565735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482886} {:answer/id #uuid "43cbc5c9-67b9-5e07-8eb7-2a5e0e21e482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482928} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9893e8-8c90-5213-9bbf-617a8a90550e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483079} {:answer/id #uuid "a38f4d63-059b-5216-94a8-f8f05c6278e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cb798f6-eb02-5eb0-b90e-3818bf565735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482886} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0979245-762e-5229-aacd-89b1d72de194" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc370c50-c95d-53af-9285-c137ac273145" :question/math-academy-id "q-336704" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into {{field-3}} ." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Let's substitute ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$ from $\\text{L4}$ into $\\text{L3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+\\cancel{AΦu(t)}=\\cancel{AΦu(t)}+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3}\\text{ and }\\text{L4}$ by substitution.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into  $\\text{L1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f0a9736-6f06-5aca-901d-8b514f93cdc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30t-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f0a9736-6f06-5aca-901d-8b514f93cdc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30t-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482979} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15a7d26-52b7-5906-9325-e15f061f4537" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21549105-6be0-5d64-8508-5994c6653a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337154" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}6t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$36t-42$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 12t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-3t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 12t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -3t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 12t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -3t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-3t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=12t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=12\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =12t\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int 12\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12t{e}^{t}-12{e}^{t} \\\\  & =12{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 12{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}6(1-2t)+48(t-1) \\\\ 9(1-2t)+48(t-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}36t-42 \\\\ 30t-39\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8039f17-10a2-5a2d-8787-b1e6d3df2d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33t-37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8039f17-10a2-5a2d-8787-b1e6d3df2d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33t-37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483096} :answer-field/id #uuid "9324a6eb-07ab-5ed4-84d0-646338f3a965" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "858d57fe-416e-56c5-af8b-4ea6e4298062" :question/math-academy-id "q-337289" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3t-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =4t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =t\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}36(t-1)-(3t+1) \\\\ -(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}33t-37 \\\\ -3t-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233241c0-123a-55a0-9903-366c8ec5a21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233241c0-123a-55a0-9903-366c8ec5a21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309015} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a284f86-7e4b-51fd-b771-60a6ffad1c2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7ef0fb5-75be-526d-82d2-8faa19bc8d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-337306" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$39t+23$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}-\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4(3t-1)+9(t+1) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}21t+5 \\\\ 39t+23\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65022199-6a0a-59fa-b71e-95b9b1843201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65022199-6a0a-59fa-b71e-95b9b1843201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528} :answer-field/id #uuid "92650cbf-bf86-5f3b-a335-e3e58d6d03f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eaf12e35-a3fc-5fd0-814c-953ea7b38802" :question/math-academy-id "q-337307" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$30t+20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
